If `old.close()` hangs indefinitely (e.g., unresponsive subprocess, broken pipe, or network stall), `release()` is never called because it executes after the `await old.close()` completes. This permanently blocks every subsequent `attach()` and `disconnect()` call, since they all chain through `await prev` on the never-resolving lock promise. The same issue exists in `disconnect()`.

Suggestion: Wrap `old.close()` with a timeout (e.g., a `Promise.race` with a 5-second deadline) so the lock is always released even if close() hangs:
```typescript
const CLOSE_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;
await Promise.race([
  old.close(),
  new Promise<void>((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('GRAPHIFY_CLOSE_TIMEOUT')), CLOSE_TIMEOUT_MS)
  ),
]);
```

